\begin_layout Section
Measuring Entanglement
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The von Neumann entropy is an important measure of entanglement in a quantum system
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The von Neumann entropy,
 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{V}$
\end_inset

,
 of a quantum system can be calculated from its density matrix
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For a quantum system that is disentangled from the rest of the universe,
 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{V}=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For a quantum system that is disentangled from the rest of the universe,
 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{V}>0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
In order to understand von Neumann entropy,
 we first understand Shannon entropy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Shannon Entropy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The Shannon entropy,
 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{S}$
\end_inset

,
 for a syststem is a measure of uncertainty in the system,
 and it is defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
S_{S}=-\sum_{x}p(x)\log_{10}\left(p(x)\right),\label{eq:EntropyShannon}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the sum is over all the possible outcomes 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 is the probability of outcome 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

