Exercise 5.14
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Suppose we had rigged a coin toss,
 and we knew that there is a 100% probability of getting 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

heads
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (1),
 and 0% chance of getting 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

tails
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (0)
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p\left(0\right)=0\qquad\text{and}\qquad p\left(1\right)=1
\]

\end_inset

Find the Shannon entropy of this system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
#+html:
 <details><summary>Show/hide solution...</summary>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The Shannon entropy for this example is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
S_{s}=-0\cdot\log_{10}\left(p(0)\right)-1\cdot\log_{10}\left(p(1)\right)=0.
\]

\end_inset

#+html:
 </details>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
#+end_note
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In the above example,
 we have zero entropy.
 This means we have:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
zero uncertainty about the outcome,
 and
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
complete knowledge about the outcome of the system
\end_layout

\end_deeper
