\begin_layout Plain Layout

\series bold
Exercise 5.15
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Suppose we have a fair coin toss,
 and the outcome is completely random:
 there is a 50% probability of getting 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

heads
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (1),
 and a 50% chance of getting 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

tails
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (0)
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p\left(0\right)=p\left(1\right)=0.5
\]

\end_inset

Find the Shannon entropy of this system.
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "exer:EntropyFairConToss"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
#+html:
 <details><summary>Show/hide solution...</summary>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The Shannon entropy for this example is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
S_{s} & =-p(0)\log_{10}\left(p(0)\right)-p(1)\cdot\log_{10}\left(p(1)\right)=-\frac{1}{2}\log_{10}\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)-\frac{1}{2}\cdot\log_{10}\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)\\
 & =-0.301
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
#+html:
 </details>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
#+end_note
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In computing and quantum computing,
 it may be desirable to measure entropy as an uncertainty in bits.
 Thus,
 we use
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
S_{S}=-\sum_{x}p(x)\log_{2}\left(p(x)\right),\label{eq:EntropyShannonBits}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

